From 84e34beb7bd84a20cd9c15782da2dccc6f2f5e99 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: OpenWCH Phase 3 <phase3@openwch.local>
Date: Sun, 16 Aug 2026 02:13:08 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 4/7] RISC-V: make the attribute section write-out opt-in

Upstream synthesises the RISC-V attribute section at finish time whenever
riscv_opts.arch_attr or an explicit .attribute directive asks for it.  The
official WCH 15.2.0 assembler does not: on the same inputs it writes no
.riscv.attributes at all, and 43 EVT objects differed from ours for that
reason alone.

Probing the official assembler shows what decides it: a long option
--w_priv_spec that it accepts, that neither --help nor --target-help
lists, that rejects an argument, and that turns synthesis on.  The
upstream condition survives underneath it, so the gate is

  w_priv_spec && (riscv_opts.arch_attr || explicit_attr)

Each factor is separately observable.  -mno-arch-attr still suppresses
synthesis with the option given, which is only possible if
DEFAULT_RISCV_ATTR is still 1 -- and that in turn is why -march-attr looks
like a no-op there: it sets a flag that is already set.  An explicit
.attribute directive keeps recording its operand with the option off, but
the operand stays verbatim; only synthesis rewrites it into canonical
form, which is where a bare xw becomes xw2p2.

A run-time flag is also the only shape that keeps the assembler whole.
TARGET_VENDOR is a compile-time string literal, so gating on it folds to a
constant: riscv_set_public_attributes loses its only call to
riscv_write_out_attrs, and the function disappears together with its
exclusive diagnostic

  internal: bad RISC-V privileged spec (%s)

which both upstream builds and the official assembler retain.  No object
under test reaches that path, so the product bytes cannot show such a
loss; only a strings differential against a matching upstream build can.

The flag defaults to 0 unconditionally rather than through a
target-dependent configure default, and that choice deserves its reasons
stated:

  * It is the whole of the observable difference.  The default is what the
    official assembler does, and every surface that can distinguish the
    two -- the presence and exact bytes of .riscv.attributes across the
    four gate combinations, --help and --target-help, the rejection of an
    argument -- was probed against it and agrees.  A configure-time
    default reached through gas/configure.tgt, in the manner of
    DEFAULT_RISCV_ATTR itself, would produce byte-identical behaviour on
    this target; it differs only in what other RISC-V targets would
    default to.

  * A configure default cannot be added without regenerating the generated
    configure script.  That regeneration is sensitive to the exact
    autoconf version, produces a diff far larger than the change it
    carries, and would put a generated file whose contents affect the
    built binaries into the series for the sake of targets this series
    does not build.

  * Nothing here is built for another target.  Writing a target
    conditional now would be speculative generality: the behaviour it
    would protect is not exercised, not tested, and not compared against
    anything.

The tests carry the consequence honestly.  Because the flag rather than
the target decides, the upstream attribute tests keep running on this
target: the invocations that need the section pass --w_priv_spec, which
also keeps their upstream expectations intact -- the priv-spec the
csr-insns-pseudo tests pass to objdump by hand, the optional
.riscv.attributes symbol in imply and option-norvc, and the gas/elf
section2 expectation, whose upstream file matches once the one invocation
that inspects the section asks for it.  Five new cases pin the quadrants
the upstream tests do not cover: the section absent by default,
-march-attr not changing that, an explicit directive recorded verbatim
with the option off, and the same directive canonicalised with it on.  The
fifth, wch-attribute-w-priv-spec-off.d, is the one that shows the second
factor still bites once the option is open: with --w_priv_spec given and
-mno-arch-attr clearing arch_attr, nothing is written.  Without that case
the gate would be indistinguishable from a single factor.

Behavior sources:
  analysis/toolchain/phase2-diff-inventory.md sections "Non-XW
  diagnostics: v3a-gpio" and "Difference classification"
  analysis/toolchain/wch-gcc-toolchain-survey.md section 5.2
Option shape, gate shape and every expectation added here were captured
from the official WCH 15.2.0 assembler.
